      value: 2
      objectReference: {fileID: 0}
    - target: {fileID: 3357063327598446276, guid: 6a3b6aa2e571ae041b78fd60af199e5e, type: 3}
      propertyPath: m_Text
      value: The American alligator (Alligator mississippiensis) resides within the
        southern extreme of the Florida Everglades. They primarily inhabit freshwater
        areas such as swamps and marshes or like, rivers and similar smalles bodies
        of freshwater. Adult male alligators occasionally reach 13 to 15 feet in
        length. Maximum length for females is approximately 10 feet. Their diet consists
        mostly of fish, turtles, small mammals, brids and reptiles. Alligators in
        general are an important part of the ecosystem as their nests serve as important
        for the creation of peat, with the adandoned nests providing a place for
        many animals to lay their eggs. Their roles as important consumers in the
        ecosystems food web also solidifies their importance to the enviornment.
      objectReference: {fileID: 0}
